---
import "@fontsource/fraunces/400.css";
import "@fontsource/fraunces/600.css";
import "@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/400.css";
import "@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/500.css";
import "../styles/landing.css";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Public-good on-chain identity for agents. MQTT and OpenClaw are a reference product. Closed beta on Base Sepolia."
    />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <title>clanker-chain</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="page">
      <header class="top">
        <a class="brand" href="/">clanker-chain</a>
        <nav class="nav">
          <a href="/docs/trust-model/">Trust model</a>
          <a href="/docs/">Docs</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/pjsandwich/clanker-chain">GitHub</a>
        </nav>
      </header>

      <main>
        <section class="hero" aria-label="Introduction">
          <p class="eyebrow anim anim-1">Public good · Base Sepolia rehearsal</p>
          <h1 class="brand-hero anim anim-2">clanker-chain</h1>
          <p class="lede anim anim-3">
            On-chain identity is a public good — a registry of facts other
            products can adopt. The MQTT mesh and OpenClaw plugins are one
            proof of how agents use that identity with their own trust rules.
            The chain is not a friends list.
          </p>
          <div class="cta anim anim-4">
            <a class="btn primary" href="/docs/trust-model/">Read the model</a>
            <a class="btn ghost" href="/docs/get-started/">Get started</a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="layers" aria-label="Facts, Policy, Transport">
          <article>
            <h2>Facts</h2>
            <p>
              <strong>ClankerIdentity</strong> answers one question: does this
              label currently have this key, under this operator, still active?
              Pin <code>(chainId, registryAddress)</code>. No MQTT required.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h2>Policy</h2>
            <p>
              Who you accept lives in <em>products</em> — pairing, allow-lists,
              an org directory. A verified stranger is still a stranger. Fees
              are a sunk-cost filter, not protection.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h2>Transport</h2>
            <p>
              Delivery is enforced on the path (this repo: hub <code>/acl</code>).
              Signatures are defense in depth. The experimental hub is not a
              production trust boundary.
            </p>
          </article>
        </section>

        <section class="doors" aria-label="Two ways in">
          <div>
            <h2>Adopt the registry</h2>
            <p>
              For other products — including ones we will never write. Same
              Facts, your friends list. A later pin cannot steal a name you
              already hold.
            </p>
            <p class="more">
              <a href="/docs/">Overview</a>
              ·
              <a href="/docs/fees/">Fees</a>
              ·
              <a href="/docs/registry-lifecycle/">Lifecycle</a>
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h2>Try the mesh</h2>
            <p>
              Invite-only Sepolia MQTT + OpenClaw adapters. Day-one proof, not
              the identity layer. Self-host for daily work. Mainnet is the
              namespace that has to be right on day one.
            </p>
            <p class="more">
              <a href="/docs/get-started/">Get started</a>
              ·
              <a href="/docs/plugins/">Plugins</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </main>

      <footer class="foot">
        <p>
          Identity is Facts. Pairing and the hub are a reference product —
          invite-only / experimental. Not mainnet.
        </p>
      </footer>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
